{"event": "web_search", "client_ms": 10005, "data": {"request_id": "f0b6fd4dab04", "seq": 11, "ts": "2026-10-27T18:11:04.293Z", "t_ms": 9771, "agent": "research", "tool": "tavily_search", "server": "tavily-remote-mcp", "transport": "streamable_http", "purpose": "find current developments on the topic", "query": "boiling point of water summit Mount Everest atmospheric pressure", "time_range": null, "status": "ok", "latency_ms": 3964, "results": [{"cite_id": "W1", "url": "https://staging.baroquehair.co.uk/signal/boiling-point-of-water-on-mount-everest-17u6pwq", "title": "Reviewing Boiling point of water on Mount Everest | Baroque Signal", "domain": "staging.baroquehair.co.uk", "score": 0.9077, "retrieved_at": "2026-10-27T18:11:00.329Z", "snippet": "The boiling point of water at Mount Everest is lower than at sea level because atmospheric pressure drops with altitude. At the summit, water typically boils around 70 °C (158 °F), though the exact temperature can vary with weather conditions.\n\n## Altitude reduces atmospheric pre"}, {"cite_id": "W2", "url": "https://www.madsci.org/posts/archives/2000-12/976811375.Es.r.html", "title": "Re: What is the boiling point of water on top of Mount Everist", "domain": "www.madsci.org", "score": 0.8763, "retrieved_at": "2026-10-27T18:11:00.329Z", "snippet": "matters for boiling. At sea level, water boils at approximately 100 degrees Celsius. In the Denver area where I live, the atmospheric pressure is about 83% of that at sea level, and water boils at about 95 degrees C. Atop Mount Everest, it is about 34% of sea level, which transla"}, {"cite_id": "W3", "url": "https://www.instagram.com/p/DOY94aICNin?hl=en", "title": "Instagram", "domain": "www.instagram.com", "score": 0.8614, "retrieved_at": "2026-10-27T18:11:00.329Z", "snippet": "Water can boil at room temperature under low air pressure, such as at high altitudes like Mount Everest’s summit (8,848 meters). Boiling occurs when water’s vapor pressure equals the surrounding atmospheric pressure, which drops significantly at elevation. At sea level, water boi"}, {"cite_id": "W4", "url": "https://www.lpi.usra.edu/education/explore/ice/background/allAboutWater", "title": "Explore - All About Ice - All About Water and Ice Background", "domain": "www.lpi.usra.edu", "score": 0.8403, "retrieved_at": "2026-10-27T18:11:00.329Z", "snippet": "At sea level, pure water boils at 212 °F (100°C).\n At the lower atmospheric pressure on the top of Mount Everest, pure water boils at about 154 °F (68°C).\n In the deep oceans, under immense pressure, water remains liquid at temperatures of 750°F (400°C) around hydrothermal vents."}]}}
